Jahshaka patch 0082 — the bloom composite takes an AMOUNT.

MEDIA-only patch (`Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/FinalToneMapping_ps.glsl`,
one file, two hunks). THE PROCESS COST IS STATED RATHER THAN AVOIDED: Studio
stages its media from the SOURCE tree (cmake/IncludeOgre.cmake), so every tree
must reset its ogre-next submodule and re-run `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` —
no engine recompile beyond the staging, but the loop must run.

IT OVERLAPS PATCH 0079 (the dither), which is the only other patch that touches
this file: 0082 adds a second member to the parameter block 0079 introduced and
edits the line above it. The two apply in number order and nothing else in the
stack goes near the HDR media, so the reverse-check's overlap class (CLAUDE.md)
covers exactly this pair — a tree that already carries 0079 and 0082 must reset
the submodule before `build-ogre.sh` rather than read the per-patch reverse
check's complaint as "upstream changed the file".

WHY (owner review 2026-09-18, item R17, lane BLOOM-AMOUNT-1): "bloom also needs
a value setting next to its check box so we can change the amount of bloom from
0 to 2x with current bloom being 1." The renderer had no strength term at all —
`bloomThreshold` and `bloomKnee` decide WHICH pixels bloom, and nothing decided
how much of the result reached the picture. The composite in this file was

    vSample.xyz += fromSRGB( bloom ) * 16.0;

and the amount is one multiply on that term.

WHY HERE AND NOT IN THE LADDER, which is the whole engineering content of this
patch. The obvious cheaper place is the bright pass, whose `brightThreshold`
constant the host already writes (chain::setBloomThreshold) and whose vec4 has
two unused components — no shader change at all if the amount could ride there.
It cannot, twice over:

  * THE BLUR TARGETS ARE R10G10B10A2_UNORM (OgreChain.cpp's own note on the
    ping-pong: the pin's FP16 bloom is slower and this ladder is fixed at
    256x256). A scale above 1 applied before that write CLIPS at 1.0, so "2x"
    would brighten the dim half of a halo and do nothing at all to the part a
    person actually looks at.
  * THIS QUAD READS THE LADDER THROUGH `fromSRGB`, WHICH IS A SQUARE. A factor
    of s applied upstream arrives as s² — 2x would be 4x, 0.5x would be 0.25x,
    and the dial would not be a multiplier in any unit the user could reason
    about.

At the composite the multiply is linear in the radiance the bloom contributes,
which is what "twice the bloom" has to mean, and it costs one multiply per
pixel in a quad that already runs.

THE UNIFORM IS THE AMOUNT MINUS ONE (`jahBloomAmountMinusOne`), following the
rule patch 0079 set in this same parameter block: the SAFE value — the one a
zero-filled constant buffer carries, i.e. any path that renders this material
without the host having pushed anything — must be the picture this engine drew
before the patch existed. Zero therefore means 1x. It is clamped in the shader
to the document's own 0..2 range, so a garbage buffer cannot subtract light.

AMOUNT 1 IS BYTE-IDENTICAL, by arithmetic and by measurement. `clamp(1.0 + 0.0,
0.0, 2.0)` is exactly 1.0, `16.0 * 1.0` is exactly 16.0 in IEEE-754, so the
multiplied term is bit for bit the term this line produced before — both of
the --engine-selftest hashes are unchanged by this patch (the lane measured them:
pose 1 2aadbc10ec831fd5e2fb4eba88d6e36e7abc008b960b6cda8e5f57e61ee6db29,
pose 2 0f084cec87ff265e2852f8f5294def97eec3d6ffdbc7ec7251449f8190866573).
Amount 0 makes the added term exactly zero, which is precisely what the
bloom-OFF chain feeds this quad (a black bloom texture), so scrubbing to zero
reaches the bloom-off picture without changing the compositor's shape.

THE HOST SIDE is `PostFxDesc::bloomAmount` → `ChainDesc::bloomAmount` →
`chain::setBloomAmount` (OgreChain.cpp), pushed per view from applyViewGlobals
beside the threshold, and from OgreView::setPostFx for the VR session's view,
which the engine's per-frame loop never reaches. A tree whose staged media
predates this patch logs one line and renders every scene at 1x.

VERIFIED: `git apply --check` against the pristine pin with 0001-0081 applied,
and `git apply --reverse --check` after applying. The pixel evidence is
tests/hdr bloom.amount (0 / 1 / 2 on a bright-emitter fixture, in one process).

